A||||13||saleenfiero||Clear Coat Woes||||5||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||04-06-2001||02:12 PM||||I have a 4X4" or so area on my back bumper right over the PONTIAC that has the clear coat gone off of it.  Can I fix this myself with some of that spray on clearcoat or what would I do?  I wanna fix it before it overruns the car.<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||5
Z||000001||KRMFiero||04-06-2001||02:16 PM||||I would not use spray paint clear coat, if i was you i would remove the bumper and bring it down to a paint shop and just have them repaint the bumper so it all matches<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steven||04-06-2001||02:45 PM||||I agree, take it to a body shop, because whatever is causing the clear coat to lift now, will cause any further paint or clear coat to lift again.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||04-06-2001||05:35 PM||||thats a typical clear coat problem. the whole bumper will have to be sanded to get any peeling clear off. then it will have to be repainted basecoat color and all recleared. Id never take the bumper off, do it on the car. Youll just mess it up trying to put it back on. I do it to cars for my dealer customers all the time. usually its the hood, roof, trunk lids that go bad.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-06-2001||07:56 PM||||Join the crowd....<P>The best fix, as everyone has said, is take it to be painted.<P>The quick fix, if you are like me and have to buy time, is to sand away all the loose stuff with really find Wet sand paper or steel wool and hit it with touch up paint then touch up clear coat.<P>It likely won't be a perfect match but it will look better that a car with dandruff.||||reg||1
Z||000005||saleenfiero||04-06-2001||08:07 PM||||Thanx for the responses, and I just want to cover it up for the time being until the car is painted, so I may try the simple way.  Like I said, it's not huge, but I've heard that before it gets out of hand.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero1_13.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Adam Draper||04-06-2001||10:04 PM||||          It would be cheaper to fix it yourself. but I guest you don't know how to do body work. If you have the right tools like air compresser, air gun a very clean garge, two heaters.maybe some dust controlers. The clear coat and hardner and paint will cost on volume and color I bought my paint for $49 but I did'nt need to buy the clear coat.   ||||reg||6
Z||000007||wkayl||04-06-2001||11:21 PM||||Do I see a bumper sticker in your future?||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||04-07-2001||10:07 AM||||a good cheap temp fix, is just sand the area with 600 wet/dry paper and prob can get by just spraying a coat of clear on the bad area with a spray can. Usually the base color is not bad. Wont look like its supposed to, but better than peeling.||||reg||1
